<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>
	<packageSources>
		<add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
		<!--
			"Local" is the shared staging feed, and it is both an input and an output on purpose.

			Every repository under d:\CommonLibrary links the same Packages folder - it points at
			a common Packages - and every packable project here sets
			<PackageOutputPath>$(SolutionDir)Packages/</PackageOutputPath>. So a package built here is
			immediately restorable by a sibling repository that depends on it, with nothing published
			first.

			That is the point. A new package does not have to go to nuget.org the moment it exists: the
			dependent repositories can be moved to the new version, built and tested against it locally,
			and only once the whole set is green does any of it get pushed. Publishing early would mean
			shipping versions that turn out to be wrong, or unlisting them afterwards.

			Two consequences follow from the arrangement rather than from a mistake, and both are
			explained under "The shared staging feed" in README.md:

			  * It accumulates. A package id outlives the project that produced it, so a rename leaves
			    the old .nupkg behind, still satisfying restores. Tools/prune-feed.ps1 reports those,
			    and removes them with -Delete.
			  * It is rewritten while it is read - a build repacks into the same directory another build,
			    or a test, may be restoring from.
		-->
		<add key="Local" value="Packages" />
	</packageSources>
</configuration>
